WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Change control is a structured quality
system process for evaluating proposed

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.610
changes before they affect
pharmaceutical operations.

3
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.240
Changes may involve equipment, facilities,

4
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.440
utilities, processes, methods, materials
or computerized systems. The goal is to

5
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.420
maintain a controlled state and avoid
unintended effects on product quality.

6
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.500
The appropriate scope of review
depends on risk, intended use and

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.240
applicable regulatory commitments.

8
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:33.000
A change request should clearly describe
the current state, proposed state,

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.090
rationale and affected systems. Examples

10
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.300
include replacing a
critical sensor, revising

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.960
a cleaning procedure or changing an
equipment component. The request should

12
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.490
identify relevant documents, equipment and

13
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.920
product families. Clear
scope helps reviewers

14
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.170
recognize dependencies
before implementation

15
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.370
rather than discovering
them after the change.

16
00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.270
Quality impact assessment
considers whether

17
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.290
a change could affect identity, strength,

18
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:04.170
purity, safety or other critical
quality attributes. The review may need

19
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.610
input from quality,
engineering, manufacturing,

20
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.090
validation, regulatory
affairs and other functions.

21
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:16.030
Regulatory submission or notification
implications depend on the market, product and

22
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.550
type of change. A technically small
modification can still have important

23
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.780
quality consequences.

24
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.650
Risk assessment considers credible failure

25
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.440
modes, existing controls
and the uncertainty

26
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.460
associated with the proposed change. It
can help determine required qualification,

27
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.750
validation, testing,
training and monitoring

28
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.480
activities. Risk tools
should support scientific

29
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.280
decision-making, not replace it. Acceptance
criteria and verification plans must be

30
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.600
appropriate to the actual
system, product and process.

31
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.570
Implementation should proceed through the

32
00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.760
authorization steps
established in the site

33
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.030
change control procedure.
Relevant prerequisites may

34
00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.560
include approved specifications,
impact assessments,

35
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.420
plans, procurement controls and training.
Emergency changes may require a defined

36
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.740
expedited process, but urgency does
not remove the need for documented

37
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.710
quality oversight. Unapproved informal

38
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.010
modifications can compromise
the validated state.

39
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.650
After authorization, the change should
be executed according to the approved

40
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.930
plan. Verification may
include installation checks,

41
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:30.070
functional testing,
qualification, process validation

42
00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:34.750
or document updates depending on impact.
Any departures should be evaluated

43
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.690
and recorded. The evidence should demonstrate
that required activities were completed

44
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.560
and that affected systems remain
suitable for their intended use.

45
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.090
Closing a change requires
appropriate review

46
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.100
of implementation evidence, open actions

47
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:55.240
and residual risks. Some changes may
require enhanced monitoring or effectiveness

48
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.550
checks after implementation.
The site should

49
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.360
update procedures, drawings, training and

50
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.220
other controlled records as needed.
A change should not be considered

51
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.540
complete simply because the new
hardware or software has been installed.

52
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.420
Consider a proposed replacement of a
differential pressure transmitter serving a

53
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.330
critical pharmaceutical
manufacturing area. The

54
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.820
team should evaluate
instrument range, accuracy,

55
00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.330
location, alarms, monitoring records and

56
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.240
potential effects on
environmental control. An

57
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.370
approved plan may require
calibration, functional

58
00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.340
verification, documentation
updates and impact

59
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.880
assessment of affected operations.
The exact checks depend on the system

60
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.530
and its qualified state.
